On Nov 26, 8:50 am, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com>
wrote:
> I hate those bones levels!

Once I am strong enough to kill a ghost in fewer turns than
it takes to go from not hungry to weak, and once I also have
the ability to uncurse things[1], I usually see bones as an
opportunity to pick up some extra stuff.  I'm particularly fond
of picking up bones from a different-role player, as even a
different role's starting items are often things you don't
otherwise have.  Several times I've picked up magic
markers from bones, and once I got silver dragon scale
mail.  Rings are another common benefit -- they're useless
cursed, but if you can identify and/or uncurse them they
become a resource.

There are exceptions, however.  Earlier in my current Barbarian
game (on my home system -- not the game on NAO), Minetown
was a bones level that scared me to death.  I went to Minetown
fairly early, hoping to get a bag of holding or at least a sack.
Ha.  I bugged out of there so fast, when I saw the barbed
devil named slinky, that in my haste I left my pet behind, and
there is no WAY I was going back for him.  I turned tail and
boogied to the main dungeon and did not look back.

This is partly because I remembered the game in question.
Although even if I hadn't a pet-named barbed devil in Minetown
would give any sane player some amount of pause, I think.

But this was a particularly egregious case.  That was the first
game wherein I got very far, and I credit Slinky with much of
that success.  I'd been playing all tourist games and had
been through a string of very early deaths to insurmountable
junk like a pack of Elbereth-ignoring rothes on dungeon level
2, and I was starting to get a bit paranoid, so I kind of hung
back and let my tame little dog named slinky do almost all
of the fighting.  I had blessed darts that game, and I threw
them when things lined up right, but slinky went in front of
me and I sort of followed behind him and ate whichever
corpses he left for me after he ate his fill.  He was a large
dog by the time I got to dungeon level three.  It was around
dungeon level six or eight that he fell into the polymorph
trap and became a barbed devil.  After that he pretty much
killed everything that moved, usually with dispatch.  It was
impressive.  Just for example, somewhere around level ten
or twelve he ran ahead of me and entered a room about
eight turns before I got there -- it was a medium-sized
throne room, and by the time I arrived it was cleared out.
I'd also noticed that he sometimes picked up armor and
put it on -- sometimes trading up later to something better,
so I was pretty sure by the time we reached the castle
(first time I ever got past Medusa!) at least half of his
gear must've been either enchanted or magical in some
way.  We'd toured the mines together to Mine's End and
back and eventually completed the Tourist quest, did
some preparation type stuff (e.g., enchanting my gear),
and headed into Gehennom.  I don't remember exactly
how far we got there, but we'd dealt with at least four
different named demons before we headed back up to
make some stash stops.  One of the stash stops was
in Minetown, and I also wanted to use the co-aligned
altar there for some reason.  I no longer remember
what exactly had killed me in Minetown, but it was
something we (Slinky and I) had already killed several
of earlier.  It might've been a mind flayer (either variety --
I was high level enough by then for almost anything to
be generated).

So anyway, you can imagine, when was on my way
into Minetown and caught a glimps of a barbed devil
names slinky -- no longer tame for having come
through the bones process -- I was, as you can
probably imagine, rather concerned.  I counted
myself fortunate to escape that level with my life
in tact.  I played without the benefit of Minetown
that game until MUCH later than usual, only going
back there when I was pretty confident I could
take a couple of hits from a barbed devil that had
advanced SEVERAL more levels than is usual
for such a best.  I believe I waited until after I had
cleaned out two more bones levels and the castle,
wished for a magic marker, and written my way
to heavily enchanted armor, as well as donating
to aligned priests until they stopped rewarding my
devotion.  I thought about not going back there at
ALL, but eventually I got my stats to the point
where I felt I could handle it -- and I did.

Nonetheless, that's the most fear I've yet felt
at seeing a particular thing in the dungeons.

> It had been an invisible shopkeeper in my case,

Yikes, I didn't even know that was possible.

